  VALUES ('7f604bf5-2f90-5f00-9160-30e9265971c4', 'social-core-lpm-synth', $J$Social-Core LPM (Synthetic)$J$, $J$Synthetic, designed-thought-experiment K-12 Learning Progression Model for the social sciences (groups, norms and rules; cooperation, conflict and collective action; social change and the diversity of societies). It uses an intentional and institutional vocabulary only and borrows no mechanism from biology or computing. It is a comparison object for LPM design research and has not been validated in any classroom.$J$, 'active', 'designed-thought-experiment', $J$Synthetic comparison object per ConceptBase RFC-0019 -- always label distinctly from in-process/field-validated projects. AI-drafted 2026-10-25 from the Social-Core and Computational-Core preparation note. No social-science or equity review has taken place.$J$, 'hosted', TRUE, 'draft', 'general', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], ARRAY['en']::text[], 'orange', $J$Status: synthetic. Nobody has taught from this space, AI drafted it from published research, and no subject-matter expert has reviewed it. Use it to compare designs and test ideas. Do not use it to plan lessons. It was built on 2026-10-25 before any social-science or equity review, so read every claim about groups, norms and societies as a draft for expert reading.

Social-Core is a thought experiment about how far an intentional and institutional vocabulary carries a student from kindergarten to grade 12 in explaining why groups cooperate, conflict, hold rules and change. Every explanation cites goals, beliefs, rules, roles, incentives, power or history. It borrows no mechanism from biology or computing, so it cannot say that a custom spread because it was selected. That gap is the point. It shows what the integrated Interdisciplinary space adds when it allows cultural selection as an explanation, and what that addition risks.

It has 15 concepts, 3 strands (Groups, Norms and Rules; Cooperation, Conflict and Collective Action; Social Change and Diversity of Societies) and 12 stages across four grade bands. It is the social member of a four-space comparison with Bio-Core, Computational-Core and Interdisciplinary. It covers a narrow part of what schools teach as social studies and has nothing on economics, geography or history as such. Its sources lean Western and English-language. How much of a real social-studies curriculum it covers is a measured result of the comparison and a known limit here.$J$)
