  VALUES ('bac697ab-febe-5111-a5bd-f266b8b2d837', 'computational-core-lpm-synth', $J$Computational-Core LPM (Synthetic)$J$, $J$Synthetic, designed-thought-experiment K-12 Learning Progression Model for computing (data and representation; algorithms and programs; systems, models and learning from data). It uses a procedural and formal vocabulary only and attributes no intention or understanding to machines. It is a comparison object for LPM design research and has not been validated in any classroom.$J$, 'active', 'designed-thought-experiment', $J$Synthetic comparison object per ConceptBase RFC-0019 -- always label distinctly from in-process/field-validated projects. AI-drafted 2026-10-25 from the Social-Core and Computational-Core preparation note. No computing-education expert has reviewed it.$J$, 'hosted', TRUE, 'draft', 'general', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], ARRAY['en']::text[], 'blue', $J$Status: synthetic. Nobody has taught from this space, AI drafted it from published research, and no subject-matter expert has reviewed it. Use it to compare designs and test ideas. Do not use it to plan lessons.

Computational-Core is a thought experiment about how far a procedural and formal vocabulary carries a student from kindergarten to grade 12 in explaining what computing systems do and how they are built. Every explanation cites data, rules, state, algorithms, specifications, models or simulation. No explanation says that a system wants, understands or intends anything, and none borrows a mechanism from biology or social science. The word agent appears only as a technical term for a process that follows rules. This guard against attributing mind to machines is the computing counterpart of the guard Bio-Core keeps against attributing purpose to organisms.

It has 15 concepts, 3 strands (Data and Representation; Algorithms and Programs; Systems, Models and Learning from Data) and 12 stages across four grade bands. It is the computational member of a four-space comparison with Bio-Core, Social-Core and Interdisciplinary. The integrated space asks when the algorithm wants is useful shorthand and when it misleads. This core has no word for wants, so it cannot ask. It covers a narrow part of what schools teach as computing, and how much of a real computing curriculum it covers is a measured result of the comparison. Only 5 of its 15 concepts trace to the Interdisciplinary vocabulary, which is thin on core computing.$J$)
